{
  "image_name": "1c9e896912a5df648778871c759f3429.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes endocrine changes in primary adrenal insufficiency and shows how the hypothalamic-pituitary axis interacts with the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Reduced adrenal output leads to low cortisol, low adrenaline, and low aldosterone. The drop in cortisol removes normal negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary, so CRF, vasopressin, and ACTH rise. High ACTH is linked to enlarged, nodular adrenal glands, but these glands still appear unable to produce adequate cortisol.\n\nOn the mineralocorticoid side, low aldosterone causes increased serum potassium, decreased serum sodium, and reduced blood volume, which together lower blood pressure. The kidney responds by increasing renin, activating angiotensinogen conversion to angiotensin I and then angiotensin II via ACE in the lungs. Angiotensin II and vasopressin seem to act as stimulatory signals in this network, but aldosterone remains low despite this compensatory activation, indicating adrenal failure rather than lack of stimulation.\n\nThe image also suggests a shift toward increased adrenal androgens despite reduced cortisol and adrenaline production. Overall, the diagram emphasizes failed adrenal hormone production with strong upstream hormonal compensation and secondary disturbances in electrolytes and blood pressure.",
